第二輪 review B2 指出、經核實：註解寫 Reverted to 256，而 #define 實際係 512，
另描述一個已被推翻嘅 fp16 回歸實驗。任何人照註解判讀都會得出「生產 = 256」嘅錯結論。

改為完整 HISTORY 區塊（256 baseline / 512 現行 / 1024 否決 / THREADS_X=128 否決），
並明寫「#define 下一行才是單一真相」。純註解改動，0 行非註解變更，
故不需重新編譯，生產 .so（7847e12eaf3d8b10）不受影響。
---
 .../aot/csrc/gemm/gptq/q_gemm_rdna3.cu        | 31 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/python/sglang/kernels/aot/csrc/gemm/gptq/q_gemm_rdna3.cu b/python/sglang/kernels/aot/csrc/gemm/gptq/q_gemm_rdna3.cu
index 76350bc..4e6b208 100644
--- a/python/sglang/kernels/aot/csrc/gemm/gptq/q_gemm_rdna3.cu
+++ b/python/sglang/kernels/aot/csrc/gemm/gptq/q_gemm_rdna3.cu
@@ -63,19 +63,26 @@
 namespace vllm {
 namespace gptq_rdna3 {
 
-// BLOCK_KN_SIZE = 256 (was 128 in exllama). Each block covers 256 K
-// elements and THREADS_X*4 = 1024 N columns. For Qwen-class K=4096 this
-// halves gridDim.z (32 → 16) and therefore halves the atomic count per
-// output position vs the exllama default. THREADS_X=256 = 8 waves on RDNA3
-// wave32; with ~32 wave slots per CU we still fit 4 blocks per CU at peak.
+// BLOCK_KN_SIZE = 512  (was 128 in exllama, then 256). Each block covers
+// 512 K elements and THREADS_X*4 = 1024 N columns.
 //
-// We tried BLOCK_KN_SIZE=512 (microbench on Qwen3.6-27B): bf16 improved
-// 5-10% at large M (atomic CAS halved), but fp16 decode regressed up to
-// +40% on qkv-square (32 → 45 μs at M=1). Cause: 16 waves/block × 16
-// total blocks for [M=1, K=N=4096] only saturates ~8 of the 96 CUs,
-// breaking memory-latency hiding for the fp16 path which is already
-// memory-bound. Reverted to 256; bf16 keeps most of its gains from the
-// fp32 dequant rewrite alone.
+// HISTORY - read before changing this value. The #define below is the single
+// source of truth; this comment used to contradict it (see NOTE at the end).
+//   256  = original RDNA3 port; kept as the rollback baseline.
+//          sha ff4d6acbc1e42aad, saved at /tmp/so_prod_rollback.so
+//   512  = CURRENT, chosen 2026-09-13. Paired microbench ratio vs 256
+//          = +3.7% (control 0.998); +7.1% on the log accept-3.0 bucket.
+//   1024 = REJECTED. User A/B on the fixed 3-turn sample: 61 t/s vs 70 t/s
+//          for 512. Split-K partial overhead = 16*M/BLOCK_KN_SIZE, so a
+//          larger block trades atomics for fewer partials; beyond 512 the
+//          extra K-loop iterations dominate at M=4.
+//   THREADS_X=128 variant measured -23%. Do not retry.
+//
+// NOTE: an earlier version of this comment claimed the value had been
+// reverted to 256 while the #define already read 512. It also described an
+// fp16 regression from a superseded experiment. Both were wrong. If you are
+// reading this to decide whether production runs 256 or 512: production runs
+// 512 (sha 7847e12eaf3d8b10), and the value is set on the next line.
 #define BLOCK_KN_SIZE 512
 #define THREADS_X 256
 
